<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><title>NYT &gt; Technology</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/section/technology</link><description>Technology industry news, commentary, and analysis, with reporting on big tech, startups, and internet culture. The New York Times is a hub for conversation about news and ideas.</description><atom:link rel="self" href="https://rss.nytimes.com/services/xml/rss/nyt/Technology.xml" type="application/rss+xml" /><item><title>United Airlines Blocks A.I. Agents From Booking Flights</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/business/united-airlines-ai-agents-booking-flights.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/business/united-airlines-ai-agents-booking-flights.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/11/business/united-airlines-ai-agents-booking-flights.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>The carrier said that to protect customers’ information and comply with regulations, it was barring the digital assistants from logging into accounts and managing reservations.</description><dc:creator>Ishani Desai</dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:15:31 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">United Airlines</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Airlines</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">airplanes</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Reservations</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Privacy</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Customer service</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Anthropic</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">OpenAI</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Google</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Meta</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">FAA</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">US</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Regulation and Deregulation</category><media:content url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/11/11/multimedia/11xp-ai-united-mpzg/11xp-ai-united-mpzg-videoSixteenByNineJumbo1600.jpg" medium="image"><media:credit>Mike Blake/Reuters</media:credit><media:description>United Airlines moved to restrict A.I. agents’ access to customer accounts amid broader concerns about autonomous software that operates without human oversight.</media:description></media:content></item><item><title>With Jensen Huang Gone, the A.I. Industry Loses Its Most Powerful Backer in Washington</title><link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/12/technology/huang-ai-policy-washington.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/12/technology/huang-ai-policy-washington.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss</guid><atom:link rel="standout" href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/12/technology/huang-ai-policy-washington.html?partner=rss&amp;emc=rss" /><description>Nvidia’s chief executive, a confidant of President Trump, successfully pushed for less regulation and open borders for chip sales.</description><dc:creator>Cecilia Kang and David McCabe</dc:creator><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:54:24 GMT</pubDate><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Artificial intelligence</category><category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_des">Compute